codemap 9
tour feature-go-back
kind flow
author ai
group features/gui
note The back and forward arrow icons in the top bar, alt+left and alt+right, ctrl+left and ctrl+right, and the mouse's back and forward buttons move back and forward through the places visited, the way a browser does: a different tour, step, focused symbol, file or centre tab, or a jump to other lines of the same file, is a place, and each comes back with the tab, line selection, scroll offsets and graph camera it was left with. Switching the centre tab is its own place. Scrolling is not. Each arrow-key walk is its own place. The reader reaches for it to return to where a jump, an xref or a search started instead of finding the place again by hand, as an editor with an LSP allows. Nothing records a place explicitly; the end of every frame compares where the reader is with where they were. The arrow keys with a modifier are left to a focused text field, where they move the caret.

